[about]
Title=03 - Key Patterns
Intro=The rules every Homer key follows, so a key can be guessed in a program you have never opened: the word gives the letter, Control does, Shift asks, Shift reverses, Alt Shift is a command with no control, the function keys follow Windows, Insert is the reader's -- with a guessing exchange and the keys that explain the keys. This walk assumes walk two.
Setup=Nothing is needed; a Homer program open to try the keys is a bonus.
Homework=Guess three keys in a Homer program you have not used, then check with Control plus F1.

[step]

Say=Every key in a Homer program is named for a word in its command, and the same word gives the same key in every program. Control plus K is Keywords in DbDo and in FileDir; Control plus F is Find in all three; F2 renames or edits in place everywhere. A key never comes from the middle of a word. In DbDo, Control plus K opens Keywords: the reader names the dialog, then the box, then the hint.
Key=Control+K
Hear=Keywords dialog
Hear=Keywords: edit
Hear=Type in text.
Note=Confirm with a live run: buildTutorials -live.[step]

Say=Control plus a letter does something. Shift plus a letter, or Alt plus a letter, asks something and changes nothing. Adding Shift to a doing key reverses or widens it: Control plus M marks, Control plus Shift plus M unmarks; Control plus O opens, Control plus Shift plus O opens converting. Shift plus Z in DbDo asks for the status and changes nothing.
Key=Shift+Z
Hear=jobs, 12 records, no filter, sorted by employer
Note=Confirm with a live run: buildTutorials -live.[step]

Say=Alt plus Shift plus a letter is a command with no control of its own: Alt plus Shift plus P plays a stream, Alt plus Shift plus R records or runs a report, Alt plus Shift plus E exports.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The function keys follow Windows and Office: F1 help, F2 edit or rename, F3 find again, F4 pick from a list or list the windows, F5 run or refresh, F7 spelling, F10 the menus, F11 the newer version, F12 the AI or the files. F4 in a DbDo field with fixed values: pick from a list.
Key=F4
Hear=status: list box, untried, 4 of 5
Hear=To move through items press Up or Down Arrow.
Note=Confirm with a live run: buildTutorials -live.[step]

Say=Anything with Insert in it belongs to the screen reader; a Homer program never uses the Insert key, so the two can never collide. Insert plus T is the reader's own: it says the window's title.
Key=Insert+T
Hear=DbDo
Note=Confirm with a live run: buildTutorials -live.[step]

Say=The rules let a key be guessed, in a program you have never opened. I name a command; the reader says the key the rules give it.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Say Status.
Key=
Hear=Shift plus Z

[step]

Say=Keywords.
Key=
Hear=Control plus K

[step]

Say=Unmark, the reverse of Mark.
Key=
Hear=Control plus Shift plus M

[step]

Say=Play Stream, which has no control of its own.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus Shift plus P

[step]

Say=Check for a newer version.
Key=
Hear=F11

[step]

Say=The keys that explain the keys, the same in every program. Control plus F1 is the Key Describer: on, every key says what it does instead of doing it.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=Key Describer On

[step]

Say=Control plus F1 again turns it off. Hotkeys, in the Help menu, lists every key three ways -- by menu, by key, by command -- generated from the program itself, so it cannot fall behind. And the menus say each command's key as you arrow them.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=No Key Describer

[step]

Say=Access letters in dialogs and menus come from the plain Windows rule: the underlined letter, the first letter of a word in the caption, one per dialog; better no letter than one from inside a word. The kit's check counts them per dialog and refuses a duplicate. In DbDo's Keywords dialog, Alt plus K, K for Keywords, lands on the box.
Key=Alt+K
Hear=Keywords: edit
Hear=Type in text.
Note=Confirm with a live run: buildTutorials -live.[step]

Say=A planned misstep. Press a key the program does not bind, and nothing happens -- no beep, no message -- because an unbound key is not a mistake; the Key Describer or the Alternate Menu tells you what the key you wanted is.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The other direction, which is how the hotkey list reads: I say the key, the reader says the command it names in each program.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus K.
Key=
Hear=Keywords, in DbDo and in FileDir

[step]

Say=Control plus F.
Key=
Hear=Filter Records in DbDo; Forward Find in EdSharp; Filter in FileDir
Say=The letter holds; the word it comes from is the program's own.

[step]

Say=Shift plus Z.
Key=
Hear=Say Status in DbDo; Zip in FileDir
Say=The one place two programs part: in FileDir, Shift with a letter acts on tagged files, because FileDir's tags came before the Say layer did. The guide names the exception so the rule can stand.

[step]

Say=Alt plus F10.
Key=
Hear=The Alternate Menu in EdSharp: every command in one list you type into

[step]

Say=How a key gets chosen when a new command arrives. The word first: its first letter. Taken? The next word of the command. All taken? A command with no control gets Alt plus Shift and the letter. A key from inside a word is never chosen; a command without a good key is renamed until it has one.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A worked case. DbDo gained Play Stream. P was Pick Value's on the grid; Play had no control of its own; so Alt plus Shift plus P -- and the recording in the player took Alt plus Shift plus R, R for Record, after Rate had the plain R. The rules decided both in a minute, and both are guessable now.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A planned misstep. Give a dialog two controls whose captions start with the same letter, and the kit's check refuses the build by name: the dialog, the two captions. The fix is a caption, not a key table.
Key=
Hear=access letters -- Filter Records: F is used by Find and Filter

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I say the idea or the key; the reader says the command or the name.

[step]

Say=Control plus F1.
Key=
Hear=Key Describer

[step]

Say=Every key three ways, from the program itself.
Key=
Hear=Hotkeys
